                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{matrix}
                  \&amp;&amp;A_1&amp;\to&amp;A_2&amp;\leftarrow&amp;A_3&amp;\to&amp;\ldots&amp;\to &amp;A_{n-3}&amp;\leftarrow &amp;A_{n-2}&amp;\to&amp; A_{n-1}&amp;&amp;\\
                  \&amp;\nearrow&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;\searrow&amp;\\
                  X&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;Y\\
                  \&amp;\searrow&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;&amp;\downarrow&amp;\nearrow&amp;\\
                  \&amp;&amp;B_1&amp;\to&amp;B_2&amp;\leftarrow&amp;B_3&amp;\to&amp;\ldots&amp;\to &amp;B_{n-3}&amp;\leftarrow &amp;B_{n-2}&amp;\to&amp; B_{n-1}&amp;&amp;\end{matrix}
          %p
            (Note that this picture is technically hiding the identity morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to X
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to Y
          %p
            We note that we must define everything pointwise for the moment, but it’s reasonable expect all of this data to lift to an actual composition functor in the colimit over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
          %p With this data in hand, we may proceed to actual constructions of the simplicial categories that we want.
          %p
            Before that, a short note on terminology: Traditionally, only the second construction is called the hammock localization, but this name comes from the shape of a natural transformation between two
            = succeed "-zigzags" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
          %h2#simplicial_localization_via_the_grothendieck_construction Simplicial localization via the Grothendieck Construction
          %p
            By the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+construction"} Grothendieck construction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^t(X,Y):\mathbf{T}\to Cat
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+category"} fibered category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}
          %p
            We describe the resulting category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)
            explicitly as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              The objects are pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t,F)
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in \mathbf{T}
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\in C^t(X,Y)
            %li
              The morphisms are pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g):(t,F)\to (t',G)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi t
                    %mo →
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:t\to t'
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:f_*F\to G
            %li
              Composition is given by the rule
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %mo ,
                      %mi g
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi f
                      %msub
                        %mo ′
                        %mo *
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f',g')\circ (f,g)=(f'\circ f,g'\circ f'_* g)
          %p
            In the sequel, we will construct the strict 2-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GrC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and with morphism categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi GrC
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)
          %p
            The first order of business is to show that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi XYZ
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee^{t,t'}_{XYZ}
            assemble first to a functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi XYZ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi GrC
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi GrC
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi GrC
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee_{XYZ}:GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)\times GrC^\mathbf{T}(Y,Z)\to GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi XYZ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee_{XYZ}
            assemble to an actual law of composition.
          %p
            We define the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi XYZ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee_{XYZ}:GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)\times GrC^\mathbf{T}(Y,Z)\to GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Z)
            as follows: On objects (suppressing subscripts), we have
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi t
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %msup
                      %mo ∨
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ,
                        %mi t
                        %mo ′
                    %mi F
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t,F)\vee (t',F'):= (t\vee t', F\vee^{t,t'} F')
          %p
            We now define the strict
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %ul
            %li
              The objects are simply the objects of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              The hom-categories are given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi GrC
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)
            %li
              The identity morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi GrC
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X\in GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,X)
              is the unique empty zigzag.
            %li
              We define the law of composition to be
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∨
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
          %p
            It’s clear from the definitions that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
            is associative and that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi id
                    %mi X
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X\vee F=F
            whenever the composition makes sense. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GrC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC
            is a
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            We define the categorical nerve:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo :
                  %mn 2
                  %mtext -
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Set
                    %mi Δ
                  %mtext -
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N:2\text{-}Cat\to Set_\Delta\text{-}Cat
            to be the canonical functor arising from the ordinary nerve. That is,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is the identity on objects, and sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)\to \nu(GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y))
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            denotes the ordinary nerve. This is well defined since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            is a right adjoint and therefore commutes with products.
          %p
            We call the simplicial category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi GrC
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(GrC)
            the simplicial localization of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            %strong> Theorem
            \: The categorical nerve
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi GrC
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(GrC)
            sending objects to objects and hom-categories to simplicial sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi GrC
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu(GrC^\mathbf{T}(X,Y))
            equips
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi GrC
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(GrC)
            with the structure of a simplicial category. Further, the morphisms of the homotopy category of this simplicial category coincide with those of the homotopy category arising from the ordinary construction using equivalence relations. That is,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ho
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi GrC
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho_C(X,Y)\cong \pi_0 (N(GrC(X,Y)))
            naturally in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi Hom
                      %mrow
                        %mi Ho
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi SSet
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(-):=Hom_{Ho(SSet)}(Ho(\Delta^0), Ho(-))
          %h2#hammock_localization Hammock Localization
          %p
            We define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_H C^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)
            to be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} varinjlim
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varinjlim_\mathbf{T} \nu(C^\mathbf{T}(X,Y))
            %em Hammock Localization
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be the simplicial category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_H C
            defined by the following data:
          %ul
            %li
              The objects of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi H
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_H C
              are precisely the objects of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %li
              The hom-spaces are precisely the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi H
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_H C^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)
            %li
              Composition is given by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu(\vee)
            %li
              The identities
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Id_X
              are those maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^0\to C^\mathbf{T}(X,X)
              classifying the unique empty zigzag
          %p
            It remains to show that the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi XYZ
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu(\vee^{t,t'}_{XYZ})
            assemble to a morphism of simplicial sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi XYZ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi H
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee_{XYZ}:L_H C^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)\times L_H C^\mathbf{T}(Y,Z)\to L_H C^\mathbf{T}(X,Z)
            “in the limit”, as it were. By the universality of colimits in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SSet
            and the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
            is a right adjoint, we have that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi H
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi H
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} varinjlim
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_H C^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)\times L_H C^\mathbf{T}(Y,Z)\cong \varinjlim_\mathbf{T} \nu(C^\mathbf{T}(X,Y)\times C^\mathbf{T}(Y,Z)
          %p
            Then to prove that everything assembles correctly, we may need to use some sort of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          %p (…)
